implementationPlan.description:
Regression test: new packages/moe-daemon/src/tools/chatPresence.test.ts, built from /mnt/d/moe-scratch/hunt-d5d58972/A/zzHuntA_chatWhoDead.test.ts (ToolTestHarness; mock postToRoleChannel).
Seed, with FRESH = now and STALE = 5 min ago:
- worker-closed: CODING, FRESH, with a cursor on #general;
- worker-idle: IDLE, FRESH, with a cursor on #general;
- worker-busy: CODING, FRESH;
- worker-stale: CODING, STALE, with a cursor on #general;
- worker-joiner: READING_CONTEXT, FRESH, the chat_join caller.
Then deregister_worker worker-closed, so it is DEAD with its recent lastActivityAt kept. Put chat_wait waiters straight into activeChatWaiters (the tools/chatChannelRef.test.ts:238-241 pattern) for worker-closed and for worker-stale, both watching #general. Clear them, and their timers, in afterEach.
Assert exact id lists, sorted:
1. DoD 1:
   - chat_who {} lists [worker-busy, worker-idle, worker-joiner], with no worker-closed;
   - chat_who {channel:'#general'} lists worker-idle (source 'cursor') and worker-stale (source 'waiting'), and never worker-closed in either pass;
   - chat_join {channel:'#general', workerId:'worker-joiner'} onlineWorkers ids are [worker-busy, worker-idle, worker-joiner].
2. DoD 2: worker-idle is in list_workers with isAlive:true, and both tools list it. That pins the shipped rule: chat_join no longer drops IDLE.
3. Parity: the chat_who {} ids and the chat_join onlineWorkers ids both equal the ids list_workers reports with isAlive:true.
4. Unchanged behaviour: worker-stale is in neither no-channel list. It is listed with a channel only through its waiter.
Use toEqual on sorted arrays, never truthiness. Edit with the seat's own editor, never a shell write.
